BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20261003T230000Z
DTEND:20261004T000000Z
LOCATION:IU CINEMA
GEO:39.168613;-86.517376
SUMMARY:Grass: A Nation's Battle for Life w/Q&A | IU Cinema Jon Vickers S
 coring Award
DESCRIPTION:In 1924\, neophyte filmmakers Merian C. Cooper and Ernest B. 
 Schoedsack\, who would later co-direct King Kong (1933)\, hooked up with 
 journalist and sometime-spy Marguerite Harrison and set off to film an ad
 venture. They found excitement\, danger\, and unparalleled drama in the m
 igration of the Bakhtiari tribe of Persia (now Iran). Twice a year\, more
  than 50\,000 people and half a million animals surmounted seemingly impo
 ssible obstacles to take their herds to pasture. The filmmakers captured 
 unforgettable images of courage and determination as the Bakhtiari braved
  the raging and icy waters of the half-mile-wide Karun River. Although ma
 ny documentary historians consider Grass second only to Nanook of the Nor
 th\, few people have actually ever seen this legendary film. This restore
 d and full-length version will astonish today's audiences with its beauti
 ful photography and heart-stopping adventure. Presented with a recorded o
 rchestral score by Patrick Holcomb that was world-premiered in IU Cinema'
 s Virtual Screening Room in 2021. [71 min\; documentary\; silent film wit
 h intertitles]  Celebrating its first decade in 2026\, the IU Cinema Vick
 ers Scoring Award is named after IU Cinema's founding director Jon Vicker
 s\; it is an IU Cinema-exclusive program produced in partnership with the
  Jacobs School of Music\, and is generously endowed by former IU Trustee 
 the Honorable P.A. Mack Jr.A Q&amp\;A with composer Patrick Holcomb and I
 U Jacobs School of Music Professor Larry Groupé\, moderated by IU Cinema
  Director Emeritus Jon Vickers\, will follow the screening.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  In 1924\, neophyte filmmakers Merian C.
  Cooper and Ernest B. Schoedsack\, who would later co-direct <i>King Kong
 </i> (1933)\, hooked up with journalist and sometime-spy Marguerite Harri
 son and set off to film an adventure. They found excitement\, danger\, an
 d unparalleled drama in the migration of the Bakhtiari tribe of Persia (n
 ow Iran). Twice a year\, more than 50\,000 people and half a million anim
 als surmounted seemingly impossible obstacles to take their herds to past
 ure. The filmmakers captured unforgettable images of courage and determin
 ation as the Bakhtiari braved the raging and icy waters of the half-mile-
 wide Karun River. Although many documentary historians consider <i>Grass<
 /i> second only to <i>Nanook of the North</i>\, few people have actually 
 ever seen this legendary film. This restored and full-length version will
  astonish today's audiences with its beautiful photography and heart-stop
 ping adventure. Presented with a recorded orchestral score by Patrick Hol
 comb that was world-premiered in IU Cinema's Virtual Screening Room in 20
 21. [71 min\; documentary\; silent film with intertitles]</p><p>  Celebra
 ting its first decade in 2026\, the IU Cinema Vickers Scoring Award is na
 med after IU Cinema's founding director Jon Vickers\; it is an IU Cinema-
 exclusive program produced in partnership with the Jacobs School of Music
 \, and is generously endowed by former IU Trustee the Honorable P.A. Mack
  Jr.</p><p><i><strong>A Q&amp\;A with composer Patrick Holcomb and IU Jac
 obs School of Music Professor Larry Groupé\, moderated by IU Cinema Dire
 ctor Emeritus Jon Vickers\, will follow the screening.</strong></i></p>
