SUMMARY:Octubafest 2026 – Øystein Baadsvik\, tuba\; Tomoko Kashiwagi
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire Andrea Clearfield: Hvati (2016)Øystein Baadsvik: 
 Concerto (2009)T. Stevens: Variations in Olden Style (1989)Anna Baadsvik:
  New Kid (2014)Øystein Baadsvik: Ordner Seg (It'll be alright) (2010)Øy
 stein Baadsvik: Tango (2012)About the ArtistsØystein Baadsvik is voted o
 ne of the top 10 brass players in history by listeners on Classic FM. He 
 presented the first TED talk on the topic of tuba and is one of the most 
 popular classical musicians on YouTube with more than eight million views
 . His multifaceted musical career as a soloist and recording artist has t
 aken him all over the world. He regularly gives master classes and clinic
 s at major universities worldwide. Since 2022\, he has served as professo
 r of tuba at the Norwegian Academy of Music. His engagements include perf
 ormances with such orchestras as the Oslo Philharmonic Orchestra\, Warsaw
  Philharmonic Orchestra\, the Taipei National Symphony Orchestra and Sing
 apore Philharmonic. Baadsvik has performed in some of the most famous ven
 ues in the world\, and 2006 marked his New York recital debut at Carnegie
  Hall. Baadsvik is an active recording artist whose CDs receive unstintin
 g praise. In their review of Tuba Works\, American Record Guide remarked\
 , "This spectacular recording establishes Baadsvik as one of the best sol
 o tubists in the world." Film composer John Williams said about Baadsvik'
 s recording of his tuba concerto: "His rendering of my concerto far surpa
 sses anything a composer might hope for." Visit baadsvik.com for audio cl
 ips and further information.Tomoko Kashiwagi is visiting lecturer in the 
 Chamber and Collaborative Music Department at the Indiana University Jaco
 bs School of Music for the 2026-27 school year. Kashiwagi is associate pr
 ofessor of piano and collaborative piano and holds the Emily J. McAlliste
 r Endowed Chair at the University of Arkansas\, where she joined the facu
 lty in 2012. She is also the founder of the Ozark Music Initiative\, a no
 nprofit dedicated to building community through music. Her work has been 
 supported by multiple Mid-America Arts Alliance grants. In 2025\, she was
  inducted into the Steinway &amp\; Sons Music Teacher Hall of Fame in rec
 ognition of her commitment and passion as piano educator. She has present
 ed multiple times at CollabFest\, the conference of the International Key
 board Collaborative Arts Society. She maintains an active performance car
 eer across the United States and internationally\, exploring a wide range
  of repertoire. She earned a Bachelor of Music\, Master of Music\, and Pe
 rformer Diploma in Piano Performance from the Indiana University Jacobs S
 chool of Music studying with renowned pianist Shigeo Neriki. Kashiwagi la
 ter became the first recipient of the Doctor of Musical Arts in Collabora
 tive Piano degree from the University of Texas at Austin\, where she stud
 ied with Anne Epperson.
