 >Anna Baadsvik: <em>New Kid (2014)<br /></em>Øystein Baadsvik: <em>Ordne
 r Seg (It'll be alright) (2010)<br /></em>Øystein Baadsvik: <em>Tango (2
 012)<br /></em><br /><strong>About the Artists</strong><br /><br /><stron
 g>Øystein Baadsvik</strong> is voted one of the top 10 brass players in 
 history by listeners on Classic FM. He presented the first TED talk on th
 e topic of tuba and is one of the most popular classical musicians on You
 Tube with more than eight million views. His multifaceted musical career 
 as a soloist and recording artist has taken him all over the world. He re
 gularly gives master classes and clinics at major universities worldwide.
  Since 2022\, he has served as professor of tuba at the Norwegian Academy
  of Music. His engagements include performances with such orchestras as t
 he Oslo Philharmonic Orchestra\, Warsaw Philharmonic Orchestra\, the Taip
 ei National Symphony Orchestra and Singapore Philharmonic. Baadsvik has p
 erformed in some of the most famous venues in the world\, and 2006 marked
  his New York recital debut at Carnegie Hall. Baadsvik is an active recor
 ding artist whose CDs receive unstinting praise. In their review of <em>T
 uba Works</em>\, <em>American Record Guide</em> remarked\, "This spectacu
 lar recording establishes Baadsvik as one of the best solo tubists in the
  world." Film composer John Williams said about Baadsvik's recording of h
 is tuba concerto: "His rendering of my concerto far surpasses anything a 
 composer might hope for." Visit baadsvik.com for audio clips and further 
 information.<br /><br /><strong>Tomoko Kashiwagi</strong> is visiting lec
 turer in the Chamber and Collaborative Music Department at the Indiana Un
 iversity Jacobs School of Music for the 2026-27 school year. Kashiwagi is
  associate professor of piano and collaborative piano and holds the Emily
  J. McAllister Endowed Chair at the University of Arkansas\, where she jo
 ined the faculty in 2012. She is also the founder of the Ozark Music Init
 iative\, a nonprofit dedicated to building community through music. Her w
 ork has been supported by multiple Mid-America Arts Alliance grants. In 2
 025\, she was inducted into the Steinway &amp\; Sons Music Teacher Hall o
 f Fame in recognition of her commitment and passion as piano educator. Sh
 e has presented multiple times at CollabFest\, the conference of the Inte
 rnational Keyboard Collaborative Arts Society. She maintains an active pe
 rformance career across the United States and internationally\, exploring
  a wide range of repertoire. She earned a Bachelor of Music\, Master of M
 usic\, and Performer Diploma in Piano Performance from the Indiana Univer
 sity Jacobs School of Music studying with renowned pianist Shigeo Neriki.
  Kashiwagi later became the first recipient of the Doctor of Musical Arts
  in Collaborative Piano degree from the University of Texas at Austin\, w
 here she studied with Anne Epperson.</p>
